        {
          "id": "commands.py#<module>:0:0$v0",
          "kind": "literal",
          "loc": {
            "file": "commands.py",
            "line": 1,
            "column": 1
          },
          "literal": "Django management commands: an entry point that is not a route.\n\nA command runs with everything the application has -- its database, its secrets, its\nnetwork -- over arguments a person typed at a shell. Nothing routes to `handle`, so\nbefore this class existed no enumerated entry point reached any of this code and every\ndefect in it was not judged and found clean but never asked about, which is the failure\nADR-009 exists to prevent.\n\nThe trust label is the other half, and it cuts BOTH ways. Somebody who can run\n`manage.py` already has the host, so a shell metacharacter they type is not the same\nfinding as one a stranger posts to a route -- reporting the two at one rank would\noverstate this one. And it is emphatically not nothing: operators paste values from\ntickets, cron entries and CI variables into these arguments, and the interpolation below\nis a real defect. So it is reported, at warning, and it does not fail a build.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "commands.py#<module>:0:0$v1",
          "kind": "literal",
          "loc": {
            "file": "commands.py",
            "line": 26,
            "column": 5
          },
          "literal": "`manage.py probehost <host> --shell` -- argparse fills `handle` in."
        },
        {
          "id": "commands.py#<module>:0:0$v2",
          "kind": "literal",
          "loc": {
            "file": "commands.py",
            "line": 28,
            "column": 12
          },
          "literal": "Check that a host answers."
        },
        {
          "id": "commands.py#<module>:0:0$v3",
          "kind": "local",
          "loc": {
            "file": "commands.py",
            "line": 28,
            "column": 5
          },
          "name": "help"
        },
        {
          "id": "commands.py#<module>:0:0$v4",
          "kind": "literal",
          "loc": {
            "file": "commands.py",
            "line": 44,
            "column": 5
          },
          "literal": "A second command, reading its option out of the keyword dictionary."
        },
        {
          "id": "commands.py#<module>:0:0$v5",
          "kind": "literal",
          "loc": {
            "file": "commands.py",
            "line": 61,
            "column": 5
          },
          "literal": "SILENT -- a class with a `handle` method that is not a command.\n\n    Nothing derives this from a management-command base, so nothing about it says an\n    operator ever runs it, and its parameter is an ordinary argument some other part of\n    the program passes. The base class is what decides, not the method name and not the\n    directory: keying on `management/commands/` would have enumerated an `__init__.py`\n    and missed a command a project keeps somewhere else.\n    "
        },
        {
          "id": "commands.py#<module>:0:0$v6",
          "kind": "literal",
          "loc": {
            "file": "commands.py",
            "line": 75,
            "column": 5
          },
          "literal": "SILENT -- a command that runs a fixed command line.\n\n    Enumerated as an entry point, because it is one. It reports nothing because nothing\n    an operator typed reaches the interpreter: every part of this was written down in\n    the source and is the same in every clone of the repository.\n    "
        }
      ],
